  "strategy": "Toward Significantly Lower Upper Bound in Step Function Optimization\n\n1. Approach and Rationale:\nOur goal is to minimize the Linfinity norm of the step function's auto-convolution relative to its L2 norm (2n*max(conv(a))/sum(a)^2). We consider several deterministic and randomized strategies that explicitly target this optimization. Central to our approach is the development of a multi-stage optimization pipeline that uses fine-tuned versions of the multiplicative mirror descent (MD) algorithm. We introduce variations in handling subgradient ramps, periodic resampling, and temperature governance to prevent premature convergence and to search more effectively among the large space of possible sequences.\n\nKey innovations over the previous program include:\n   - Staged learning where subgradient intensity adapts with a temperature schedule\n   - Resampling moves targeting the entire distribution of significant peaks rather than a fixed subset\n   - Progressive long-range optimization through bridge formulations that stabilize the update sign and magnitude\n   - Tighter time budget allocation between stochastic and deterministic phases\n\nWe begin by initializing sequences across several base lengths (128, 256, 384, 512, 768, 1024) with four distinct distributions:\n   a) Bell-shaped function with flatter top\n   b) Gaussian with reduced spread (smaller sigma_frac)\n   c) Uniform function\n   d) More concentrated asymmetric function\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nWe minimize the objective F(a) = 2n * max_k |conv(a,a)[k]| / (sum a)^2.\n\nConducting the optimization we will enforce:\n   - Sum consistency: all candidates maintain sum(a) \u2248 1 (but we'll allow near-enforcement in donation operations)\n   - Non-negativity: a_i \u2265 0 for all i\n   - Bounded coefficients: each a_i < 1000 to prevent saturation\n\nEach optimization step follows the equation: a_{t+1} = a_t * exp(-\u03b7 * g_t), where g_t is the subgradient direction.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   We run the optimization for up to 1800 seconds in a deterministic, multi-stage system:\n\n   Phase0: Initialization\nLook at the parent state, height_sequence_1. Feed it into a left-envelope with a very smooth project towards a normalized Gaussian base.\n\n   Option1: 'gauss_proj_exp5' handles this explicitly...\n\n   Phase1: Main Optimization Loop (MD)\nFor 2000 steps at most, we perform:\n\n   for i in range(max_steps):\n        Compute conv=a*a via FFT\n        Identify top-k peaks (k\u2248min(20, length)) with their indices (secondary indices)\n        Compute g = - (smooth_heaviside(log-a) * conv_gradient) but using a carefully designed smooth ramp function\n        Calculate dilation magnitude from \u03b7 (cooling phase) and temperature scaling\n        If the donation magnitude is below tolerance, consider moving to stage1\n        Incorporate stochastic cooling by random reset with probability 0.1 for each step (bridge 50)\n        Break out to stage2 if no significant reduction was achieved over a period\n\n   Phase2: Fine Search (PSMP)\nPerform a coordinated descent that specifically donates excess mass away from peak positions.\n\n   for i in range(3000):\n        Compute current top peaks distribution\n        Build the effective donation mask (a_mask) that reveals the critical indices that high the profiles\n        We propose: new_sequence = bridge(bridge(bridge(old_a, new_mask), temperature))\n        But require precise gradient tracking...\n\n   Phase3: Stochastic Projection\nIf the current candidate is of a given length (1024) and earlier donors were getting stuck, we will branch to a smaller base (512) and run MD with unmatched cooling intensity.\n\n   We must return to the temperature bounds... and sum normalization.\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations:\nWe choose several base parameterizations:\n\n   Gauss styles: sigma_frac \u2208 [0.25,0.35] (picking very localized wave to focus the energy)\n   Bell styles: width_frac \u2208 [0.4,0.55] with anti-gradients that allow a flatter top without \n                 convolution peaks (much more precise direction in the second phase)\n   Uniform normals: probability simplex normalized to sum=1\n   Asym cusp: exponential decay gets faded weight instead of meaningful jumps\n\nWhen to try which: \n\n   - For the initial run (Phase1), we use {'log_base': 2, 'sigma_frac':0.25, 'width_frac':0.5} as default\n   - Then, if the temperature trend is increasing too slowly (more than 500 steps above 1e-1) we will move to early bridge (tooled bridge)\n   - If donation is falling (gradient shows it, with 95% confidence) we reset with correlated noise\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   We break the time into phases:\n\n   - Synthetic-budget computation: we estimate 5 steps per minimization, but adaptive pause on clock when running the actual MD solver says 'we run 1800 steps per run'\n   - Each FFT convolution takes O(length*log(length)) but our length is up to 1024.\n   - We must ensure we have enough time to do 5000 MD steps at most.\n\n   We'll set up an adaptive scaling:\n\n   ```\n  base_time = time_limit * 0.8\n  steps_per_phase = int(1000 + base_time * 2)\n   ```\n\n   We must reset if the objective does not step down before another phase.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\nFor every candidate, we compute the objective via the challenger function:\n\n   score = float(evaluate_sequence(candidate))\n\nIf the score is infinity, we skip. Otherwise, we compare with the current best_object.\n\n   We return the best candidate found throughout the run.\n\n   # Unveil the multi-phase operation\n   When no candidate is current, we sample from the defined pool:\n\n   try:\n        # We do a hot-start with the parent sequence if available\n        seq = height_sequence_1\n    except:\n        # Or initialize from our own 'best_init' pattern\n        pass\n\n   # Final candidate must be of length \u2264 1024 (for commercial reasons)\n   candidate_length = 1000\n   if seq is not None and length in [candidate_length]:\n        candidate = seq\n    else:\n        # Or generate a synthetic candidate from scratch\n        pass\n\n   # After optimization, we'll normalize and cap (this function already handled) \n   candidate = normalize(candidate, 1)\n   if max(candidate) > 1000:\n        candidate = 1000 * (candidate / max(candidate))\n        candidate = normalize(candidate, sum=1)\n\n   # Then do not forget to project the heat profile!\n   If the convolution turns out with high peaks, we'll cool it right down (reset)",
